        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "Last week, more than 260 researchers convened in Milan to discuss the opportunities, challenges and risks involved in scaling â€œcarbon dioxide removalâ€ (CDR) to help curb climate change. The conference â€“ held on the campus of the Politecnico di Milano â€“ is the fourth in a series, with previous editions held in Oxford, UK in 2024 , and Gothenburg, Sweden in 2018 and 2022.&nbsp; A broad range of academics â€“ from forests, oceans and soils experts through to social and political scientists â€“ discussed the co-benefits and trade-offs involved in drawing down CO2 from the atmosphere at scale, as well as the ways policy could drive CDR deployment. Dr Soheil Shayegh , director of the industrial and planetary carbon cycle programme at the Euro-Mediterranean Center on Climate Change (CMCC), told Carbon Brief the idea behind the conference was to â€œbring scientists together to convey a message to policymakers about where the technology standsâ€. He continued: â€œWe should be very clear that there still are huge uncertainties about the effectiveness of lots of this CDR technology â€“ are they marketable or not? But what is clear for us is the need for CDR.â€ Dr Morgan Edwards , the lead author of the recently published â€œ state of CDR report â€, told delegates that meeting the Paris Agreementâ€™s 1.5C goal by the end of this century would require CDR to â€œscale up rapidlyâ€ from 2.2bn tonnes of CO2 (GtCO2 per year) today to 8.8GtCO2 by 2050. She added: â€œWe need to see an upscaling in ambition over the next few years to get on a track consistent with these long-term scenarios.â€ Below, Carbon Brief summarises the key talking points at the conference. Overshoot The removal of carbon from the atmosphere is seen as crucial to coƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;k2gQE0xiv5Nd8leMVuYeNHxeGyoTSWhnEvDhxgqp4hkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurrentƒdpairƒhconstan