        "object": "It is Sunday, October 12, 2025, in Sucupira Market. Sitting in the shadow of the EstÃ¡dio da VÃ¡rzea and the plateau of Praia, temporary stalls are encroaching onto the street, full of merchants selling everything from fruit to electronics. Eventually, two nondescript minibuses pull up to the market, and the Cabo Verde national team disembarks. They will be playing the most important match of their lives in 24 hours, for a chance to play at the World Cup. But at the moment, they are relaxed and enjoying themselves. Some greet a number of the women at the stalls and take photos with some youngsters. Others chat with the passersby who join them as they walk down the street over the next hour. Hours earlier in the Hotel Perola, the players spent their morning with family. Nuno da Costa could be spotted speaking with an elderly relative in the lobby. Vice-captain Vozinha was giving tickets to a kid who had made him a poster. There is an air of some friends at a spa on holiday rather than a group of athletes on the verge of the biggest moment of their career. But this is Cabo Verde, the national slogan â€œNo Stressâ€ is ubiquitous and inescapable. From the murals of the nationâ€™s great poets on the Rampa dos Poetas, to the football shirts donned by every other person emblazoned with the number 10 and the name â€œNo Stress,â€ Cabo Verde is a nation that takes relaxation seriously. For Roberto â€œPicoâ€ Lopes, that philosophy carries onto the football pitch. â€œWe have a saying, â€˜No Stress.â€™ Thatâ€™s actually the national motto, morabeza . Itâ€™s the national sort of slogan,â€ the Irish-born defender told Africa Is a Country ahead of the crunch match against Eswatini. â€œItâ€™s probably something I had to adapt to because obviƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;f1iocfbdiQvVvT7e9X9HPnSF3VvLmpKM8R7fm6PR5lIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurrentƒdpairƒhconstant‚db